// rate.mn: the course's sample rate, 48,000 samples a second, set in one place
//
// The clock is the `Sample(48000)` effect (lib/dsp/clock.mn), and the rate
// is pinned here, at the three functions that read the clock: `Sample(48000)`
// on each states which instance of the effect the course performs, and every
// lesson that calls them carries that instance in its projected row without
// writing it down. `play` is the course's render loop, one frame per tick
// up to the last frame. Its `!Alloc` also applies to the frame it
// is handed (SYNTAX §«Negation in `with` clauses»): a lesson whose frame
// allocates is rejected at the call, and the error names this declaration.
import dsp/clock

fn rate() with Sample(48000) = sample_rate()

fn now() with Sample(48000) = current_sample()

fn advance() with Sample(48000) = advance_sample()

// How many frames `s` seconds last at the installed rate.
fn frames_of(s) = rate() * s

// The time, in seconds, the clock has reached.
fn time() = float_of_int(now()) / float_of_int(rate())

// The render loop: the frame is handed the index of the sample it renders,
// and the clock advances after it. It is tail-recursive, so the emitted code
// is a loop.
fn play(frames, frame) with !Alloc + !Sample(44100) = if now() >= frames {
  ()
} else {
  frame(now())
  advance()
  play(frames, frame)
}
